1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:11,500
Dr. Hannah Brooks: Thanks for joining at short notice. This is a safety review for the CARDIA-2 wearable. We've had five reports of skin irritation from the PulseTrack strap in the last two weeks.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Priya Nair: I pulled the data before the call. All five are from sites using the original silicone strap. None of the patients on the fabric strap reported anything.

3
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:36,000
Dr. Hannah Brooks: Severity matters most here. Four were mild redness that cleared within two days of removing the device. One patient at Site 7 had a blistering rash and saw a dermatologist.

4
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:47,000
Tom Becker: The blistering case meets the definition of an adverse event related to the device, so it has to be reported to the IRB within ten working days. I'll file that report by November 14.

5
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:58,000
Sofia Alvarez: Do we pause wearable use? Patients are already worried after the sync problems, and I don't want people taking the device off for good.

6
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:12,000
Dr. Hannah Brooks: No pause. The rate is low and the reactions are mild except one. Decision: we switch every patient to the hypoallergenic fabric strap, starting with the sites that reported cases.

7
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:22,000
Priya Nair: PulseTrack confirmed they can ship fabric straps for all 131 enrolled patients. Delivery to the sites is about ten days.

8
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:33,000
Dr. Hannah Brooks: Good. Priya, please track strap replacements site by site on the data completeness dashboard you already built.

9
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:40,000
Priya Nair: Will do. I'll add a strap status column to the dashboard by November 10.

10
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:55,000
Sofia Alvarez: I'll send a short patient message through the app explaining the new strap and how to wear it. No health details in the text, same rule as the SMS reminders. I'll have the wording ready by November 7.

11
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:06,000
Tom Becker: One open question: does switching the strap count as a change to the device? If it does, we may need to notify the FDA, not just the IRB. I'll check with the manufacturer.

12
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:14,000
Dr. Hannah Brooks: Let's get an answer on that before the steering committee on November 24. Thanks everyone.
